@*
    Task 10.1 — the ASP.NET Core replacement for this plugin's web.config
    <system.web.webPages.razor><pages> element (Requirement 4.4).

    THIS FILE IS PART OF THE PLUGIN RECIPE. Every plugin that ships a .cshtml needs one.
    All 20 plugins ship a byte-identical web.config declaring exactly two things:

      pageBaseType="Nop.Web.Framework.ViewEngines.Razor.WebViewPage"
          -> the @inherits directive below. It is what makes T("Some.Resource") resolve — the
             localizer lives on that base class — and what makes the @model type resolve
             without a fully-qualified name. Deleting the web.config without adding this file
             produces one CS0246 per @model plus one CS0103 per T(...) call, the same cluster
             tasks 7.3 and 8.4 measured for Nop.Web and Nop.Admin.

      <namespaces> = System.Web.Mvc, System.Web.Mvc.Ajax, System.Web.Mvc.Html,
                     System.Web.Routing
          -> the @using directives below, translated exactly as tasks 7.3 / 8.4 translated
             the same four entries:
               System.Web.Mvc      -> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc
               System.Web.Mvc.Html -> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Rendering (IHtmlHelper
                                      extensions), which is ALSO where SelectListItem lives
               System.Web.Routing  -> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing
               System.Web.Mvc.Ajax -> NO counterpart. The Ajax.* helpers do not exist in
                                      ASP.NET Core; verified zero "Ajax." occurrences across
                                      all 38 plugin .cshtml files. Dropped.

    WHY IT SITS AT Views/ AND NOT AT THE PROJECT ROOT. Razor discovers _ViewImports.cshtml by
    walking UP from the view's own directory — the trap task 7.5 measured as deferral 7.5-1.
    This plugin has exactly one view tree, Views/, so one file at its root reaches every view.
    It must be inside the tree covered by the project file's Content/Link glob
    (Views\**\*.cshtml), because imports are matched by the LINKED path: with the glob applied
    this file compiles as /Plugins/DiscountRules.CustomerRoles/Views/_ViewImports.cshtml and
    the view as /Plugins/DiscountRules.CustomerRoles/Views/Configure.cshtml, i.e. same
    directory. MEASURED on a probe (a base type declared only here was observed on the
    compiled view type), not assumed — if the two were linked inconsistently the imports would
    silently not apply and the failure would look like a missing model type.
*@
@inherits Nop.Web.Framework.ViewEngines.Razor.WebViewPage<TModel>

@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Html
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Rendering
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing

@*
    Nop.Web.Framework carries HtmlExtensions (Html.NopLabelFor / NopDropDownListFor /
    FieldIdFor), ViewCompatibilityExtensions (JavaScriptHelper.Encode, Html.NewViewData,
    TemplateInfo.GetFullHtmlFieldId) and ChildActionExtensions (the @Html.Action bridge,
    promoted here by task 8.3). One import covers all of them — which is why no plugin view
    call site needed a new @using for those.
*@
@using Nop.Core.Infrastructure
@using Nop.Web.Framework
@using Nop.Web.Framework.UI
@using Nop.Plugin.DiscountRules.CustomerRoles.Models

@*
    Tag helpers. 3.90 had none (they do not exist in MVC 5). Added for the same reason task
    7.3 added them to Nop.Web: the framework's form/validation tag helpers are the supported
    route to an anti-forgery token, and Html.BeginForm uses the same infrastructure.
*@
@addTagHelper *, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers
